  "content": {
    "id": "p9-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Find the smallest prime number that can be written as a sum in all three of the following ways. Show a representation for each way and explain why no smaller prime number satisfies all three conditions.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p9-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Two different prime numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$31=2+29$.",
          "worked": "$$31=2+29.$$\n\nBoth summands are prime. Since $31$ is odd, any representation as two primes must include $2$. The smallest sum of four different primes is $2+3+5+7=17$, so smaller candidates for the common prime are $17,19,23,29$. For these candidates, subtracting $2$ gives $15,17,21,27$ respectively. Only $19$ passes the two-prime condition.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Three different prime numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$31=3+5+23$.",
          "worked": "$$31=3+5+23.$$\n\nAll three summands are different primes. The remaining smaller candidate also satisfies this condition, since $19=3+5+11$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q10-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Four different prime numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$31=2+5+11+13$.\n\n$31$ is the smallest prime satisfying all three conditions.",
          "worked": "$$31=2+5+11+13.$$\n\nAll four summands are different primes. Four odd primes have an even sum, so a representation of $19$ would have to include $2$. The other three different odd primes would then sum to $17$. If the smallest is $3$, the other two must sum to $14$; the possibilities $5+9$ and $7+7$ do not give different primes. If the smallest is at least $5$, their sum is at least $5+7+11=23$. Thus $19$ fails this condition.\n\nTherefore $31$ is the smallest prime satisfying all three conditions. It is prime because neither $2$, $3$ nor $5$, the primes not exceeding $\\sqrt{31}$, divides it.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
